er  this year  so these are the kinds of things that we  aim to be able to see and and bring out  with our data all right so let me talk  about what my team does at Planet and  that's specifically turning all of these  images that we get into maps to to be a  coherent global view of the surface of  the earth alright so this is a picture  of the surface of the earth right so  this might be what you think of when you  when you think of what does the earth  look like from space this is what you  see on all these web maps and what we  all know of as is what the earth looks  like right but this is actually a very  artificial product this is this one  isn't ours this is from the Blue Marble  generated by NASA from modis satellite  data I'll show you examples of ours in  in a few minutes  but this is not actually what the earth  really looks like from space first of  all we don't see through the oceans you  don't see the the ocean topography but  most of all you don't see most of the  surface of the earth either so this is  what the earth actually looks like on  any given day right this is an image  from this week from the modis terra  sensor just just taken over the course  of one day so this is this is a typical  day on earth  in July alright so this is this is what  it looks like so how do we how do we  deal with that we're taking our images  all the time everywhere but but if we  had even if we image everything on the  earth on this day you can see well  you've got clear patches a few clear  patches in North America you got North  Africa a few places in South America  Australia  not much in Europe you know some parts  of Asia but not not the North so it's  it's a little bit messy so so our job is  to turn that into one single coherent  picture  all right so let me let me just talk  briefly about the whoa the tools we use  to build mosaics since this is a PI data  conference we'll talk a little bit about  the relevant PI data tools so everything  that we use in our in our workflow is  based on open source tools so we run our  whole pipeline in Python with various  parts in compiled C C++ as well the  heavy lifting of the computation is in G  del G del is the geospatial data  abstraction library if you've done any  any raster manipulation in open source  tools you probably use something based  on G del Frank Warmerdam the the  original author maintainer of juda also  works at planet labs so he's the  architect of our our pipeline as well so  we have a lot of help on that and then  in addition to those tools we use many  of the standard tools in the Python  stack numpy and Phi PI and selected  image and then wisteria which is a set  of wrappers around the GDL library that  are more pythonic for various other  things we use use many of the other  tools you're familiar with in the PI  data stack as well but these are the  main ones that are actually in the  production in our pipeline so happy to  talk about how we use some of these if  you're interested first of all let me  just talk a little bit about how we do  our our mosaicing so just in general  there's there's two main problems that  you have to do that they have to solve  in order to build a seamless cloud-free  mosaic of the world  those are scene selection and color  normalization just to regularize the  scenes to put them together so first  scene selection every every scene that  we're taking each individual take from a  satellite is roughly 20 20 kilometers  wide by  ten kilometers long and so it's a it's a  rectangle of a couple hundred square  kilometers and we're taking many  thousands of these a day so we if we're  making a mosaic from some time period we  have to select all the images that are  available in first for search all the  images are available for a particular  location then we have to rank them  according to clouds and other metrics  sharpness other sensor quality metrics  and I choose the best ones that we have  available in order to stitch them  together into one seamless picture so  here's an example we might have 25  images to choose from that overlap this  this tile that we're building in various  ways we select the two or three best  ones that are going to fit together to  give us an image in this case over  central Beijing and stitch them together  too to make them one piece all right so  that's that's one hard problem we've  we've got some cloud metrics we've got  some sharpness metrics and we might also  ranked by the the target date how close  it is to the to the center of the time  window and things like that but but once  we selected the scenes then that's  that's only part of our work even if  we've selected them well then we have to  do some normalization on those scenes  and then this is an older image that  shows some of our older satellite data  that has a smaller footprint but it  shows the variation in in colors and  different satellites but especially in  things that are taken on different days  so just due to atmospheric variations  you get huge differences in appearance  of the of the earth taken through  different conditions of haze mostly in  the early days there also some  differences in the filters that were  that are on the satellites that we're  taking images through now the production  satellites are pretty uniform so that's  much less of an issue  but but these atmospheric conditions  still still matter so you can see some  look blueish and some look pinkish and  and you know there's not the same color  curve applied to all these things we  have to turn this into some regular  appearance of what we we think the  surface of the earth should look like so  I'll talk about the one method reviews  to do that it's called histogram  matching this is a pretty common  normalization method from photography so  you have a color target and you have a  reference image in this and you have a  source image the image that you're  trying to regularize so so you want to  tune that that source image to match the  colors of the reference image you do  that by shifting the the histograms of  each color that is each band in this  case red green blue visible bands too to  match the the cumulative distribution of  occurrence in that and that that's a  good way to to normalize things in  general there are some issues with that  though and that is one of the biggest  issues is things change on the ground  and if your color target what you're  matching things to isn't exactly what  things look like now you're going to  make mistakes so here's here's an  example you've got a color target that  is brown you know so say it's it's a  fall color target and the hillsides are  brown and matching blindly to that brown  color target you get something sort of  washed out and brown but if you look at  what the image actually has there's snow  in this valley alright so so that  histogram matching is a pretty poor  match to to actually see what's on the  ground and one of the things we're  interested in is change so to make that  change visible using static color  targets will mask some of that  make it harder to see so we are we've  developed a new method that that's  better at showing at regularizing images  without changing their content as much  so this is a parametric [ __ ] that we've  made for the same image that that still  matches the brown of the hills and the  trees without leaves but brings out the  white and the valleys as you'd expect  with snow in the ground so that  parametric fit is well I'm not going to  talk about it in any detail but if  you're interested there is a talk at  Syfy in Austin next week I'll give you  the schedule for that at the end of this  talk but that's that's a more principled  way to do this this matching in a robust  way that's not going to distort the data  and just regular eyes things in ways  that we understand okay so that's that's  how we solve the normalization problem  in general we also use color targets too  we build our own color targets to decide  what what we're going to normalize to so  a nice source of color targets is the  Landsat 8 sensor so this is a global  summer color target so summer northern  hemisphere southern southern hemisphere  minimum snow and ice for the world we  also build other seasonal targets to  match different times so this is this is  one of our sources for for truth for  what the surface of the earth looks like  at a given time or in this case at a  broad range of time since the  hemispheres don't match alright and then  one other thing we have to do even with  the normalization there's still the  possibility of seam lines so here we've  got two different  well three different scenes that are  visible in individual images this image  doesn't quite match this image doesn't  quite match that image even though we've  done the color balancing and they're  roughly the same this image is a little  bit hazy but it  said you know the more or less  normalized in color but there's still  these seams visible so one more step we  do that we call gradient blending you  might think of this as feathering in  some other context but but to eliminate  those seen lines we essentially treat  this as a free bound well as a boundary  condition that we match values across  this seam and and we solve it as a  partial differential equation to smooth  that out and that's our our seamless  gradient blending so that's the last  piece of how we put everything together  now let me show you some examples so  here is our global summer base map so  this is sort of our best of all time  imagery summertime imagery and the  Northern Hemisphere and in the southern  hemisphere with a little little bit  broader and the input and the tropics to  be able to do that but but here we've  mapped something like 95% of the surface  of the earth we're missing mostly very  high latitude ice in well Antarctica and  in Greenland places that we don't  rectify the images very well there isn't  a lot of ground control to do t